WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.150 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone thank you for having me my  name is max  guenis and i'm uh the founder and  president of the ubi center  we are a think tank and i'm excited to  share with you  what we're about and some analyses that  we've done using github  python and jupiter so first of all  who are we we are think tank and our  mission is to produce  open source research that informs a  robust policy debate around  the idea of universal basic income  and this is a policy idea that everyone  in this society  would get an equal amount a cash grant  from the government  usually on monthly basis  and especially excited to share with you  because our origins are really rooted  in open source personally i worked at  google as a data scientist  for most of my career and throughout  that time i became increasingly  interested  in economics economic policy poverty  and how policies like ubi could  improve situations like poverty and  inequality  but i didn't really feel i had the tools  to explore that until i found a group  that  that works on this specifically called  the policy simulation library  and this is a group of economists that  builds tools open source  all in python to understand public  policy making especially economic policy  so i became really interested in using  these tools for some of my ideas  and also contributing to them long story  short i left google  pursued my graduate degree in economics  and started the ubi center about a year  and a half ago  our first paper our sort of humble  beginnings really rode the wave of andre  yang who was a  presidential candidate for democratic  primary and he focused his campaign  around  a ub version of ubi he called the  freedom dividend  it was a thousand dollars a month given  to every adult funded largely by a  value-added tax as well as some changes  to  other taxes and welfare programs um so  we did the only distributional analysis  out there of this policy um showing that  is  in fact quite progressive with the  bottom decile over doubling their  income and the top decile losing out  now we have researchers all over the  world looking at ubi from a variety of  angles in the us canada australia the uk  and pakistan obviously open source has  enabled this kind of collaboration which  has been really fruitful  and exciting a few of the projects i'll  talk about today  include simulating a child allowance  across u.s states so this is basically a  ubi for kids  as well as comparing uh the 600 per week  emergency unemployment benefits this was  part of the pandemic relief package  in the u.s to a budget neutral ubi  and finally some dynamic macro  macroeconomic modeling  of ubi so first on child allowances  i think the background here that's  pretty important is that research has  been very consistent  showing that child poverty has really  deleterious effects on a variety of  outcomes  educational attainment lifetime earnings  health  both even child mortality as well as  long-term  life expectancy so a lot of countries  especially in europe  have enacted a child allowance so for  example finland and ireland  give every parent a monthly stipend  every month for every child that they  have  and it has bipartisan appeal in the u.s  because on the conservative side they  like the  family focus on the liberal side it's  more about reducing child poverty  and it's been actually a part of the  democrats heroes act which is this  the round two version of the kobit nine  team recovery after the  the cares act so what we've done  is we've simulated different levels of  child allowances  um here i'll show you a deficit funded  version where we also have some  tax funded versions and it's pretty much  the same story  so for example california today has the  highest child poverty rate in the  country  21 and you can see how as you implement  a child allowance the child poverty rate  continues to fall  so for example at 300 dollars per month  it has fallen to  10.5 percent it's been cut in half and  if you have 500  per month child allowance child poverty  falls by two-thirds to seven percent  we also have a tool where you can select  any given state  so the u.s or any other state and it  shows the child poverty rate as a  function of  the child allowance as well as the  overall poverty rate uh some other  analyses as well  are part of this deep dive into child  allowances  so this is i think important from a  policy level because you could imagine a  child allowance that's  national there could also be states that  try to implement child allowances to  reduce  child poverty in their own state and  this empowers people to understand the  facts of those policies  so some of the tools we used uh plotly  for  all the visualization also micro df is a  package that we created to work with  microeconomic data  which involves a lot of weighted  calculations that aren't built into  a lot of the default python data  analysis packages  this is all published with a tool called  jupiter book which  we've been really enjoying working with  and it's all hosted on github so totally  open source  next analysis we uh ran is um  looking at the 600 per week unemployment  benefits that ran from april to july  2020  this was one of the most controversial  also a very generous  program that the u.s had as part of the  cares act so  we simulated extending the policy which  um  there's been a lot of appetite for um  comparing to  also a budget neutral ubi so giving  everyone a smaller amount  but with the same aggregate cost looking  backwards from 2009 to 2018 where we  have  the best data on poverty this was a  little bit more involved in terms of  technical analysis because it required  modeling the unemployment benefits as  taxable so we use policy simulation  libraries tax calculator for that as  well  and our results essentially showed that  compared to extending the  um the unemployment benefit  we looked at two alternatives so that  extending it was the gray line  and this is looking at the change in the  poverty rate we also looked at the  payroll tax cut which has been a  idea floated by president trump at the  time and two versions of a ubi  so the dark blue is a ubi to everyone  light blue is  payment to adults only and these are all  budget neutrals so the same total cost  but the payment to everybody actually  reduces poverty 20  more compared to the unemployment  benefit and this was in 2009 which  really has the most similar economic  characteristics to  today in terms of very high unemployment  really poor economic circumstances  same results if you look at the poverty  gap which is another measure of poverty  that  counts how far below the poverty line  different people are  and also inequality so sort of on every  distributional  metric a ubi is actually more  progressive than extending the  unemployment benefit  largely because there's a lot of people  who are outside the labor force who  don't benefit from the unemployment or  people who are low wage who also don't  benefit  finally we looked at dynamic  macroeconomic modeling which would be  really important if  ubi were to get to the point where  there's a law a legislation that's  proposed  so any major reform which ubi would  definitely meet that bar  it would require probably a large number  of tax reforms as well  is scored by the congressional budget  office and potentially the  joint committee on taxation so there are  some non-government models that do  dynamic modeling as well penn morton  budget model is one  and there's also the policy simulation  libraries ogusa  open source model all these do what's  called overlapping generations models  where every person in the economy is  optimizing for  their lifetime utility so how much they  want to consume in each period or  request to their kids they're basically  going to make  choices in every period around their  labor force participation how much they  save  how much they consume and all this  affects  aggregate metrics like gdp labor  labor supply investment etc  so we found some interesting results  when we simulated ubi for example  uh consumption increased most for the  poor when they're young  and the rich when they're old um and  overall it's progressive  the this was based on a pre-experimental  implementation of ubi in og usa based on  taxes  and we found some problems with that so  i'm really excited that we now have a  research assistant  who is implementing ubi directly into  this program so  we're giving back to the open source  community that way  so our major goals for the future of  course are our number one one is to  inform policy makers and the public  about the economics of ubi  but not too far behind that we really  want to build and contribute to open  source  economics tools so contributing to  groups like the policy simulation  library  as well as our own tools like micro df  and to demonstrate how open source can  improve  policy analysis so that other think  tanks or maybe new think tanks like ours  can consider the approach to not just in  terms of short term getting more  eyeballs and  you know reviews on different analyses  but hopefully recruiting people like me  who may have an interest in economics  and policy making  either if they want to change their  whole career or just have a small  bite-sized project that they can get  taste for the realm i think that'll  really improve  the whole domain you'll improve open  source and ultimately  the public who are most affected by  our public policy so with that uh thank  you very much and i look forward to your  questions  you

